Chapter 2: Day 1
Summary:

    Anna shows up in Light rainy season.

Chapter Text

"Uh, god, my head." Anna sat up, propping herself up on her elbow and rubbing her eye with the palm of her other hand.

She looked around. She didn't recognize where she was.

"How the hell did I get here?"

She was in a field on the edge of a forest. The grass seemed too tall for spring. She stood up on her unwilling feet and wobbly legs, scanning the trees for any one else's left over marking tape. She couldn't see an obvious trail anywhere.

"Where the fuck am I?" She didn't recognize the tress at all. She didn't recognize the type of tree even.

She couldn't hear the creek that she used to orient herself. The one that ends almost at the trail head.

"What the actual fuck?" She wandered up and down the tree line, searching for any sort of obvious trail. The closest thing looked like a rarely used trail, or maybe even a deer trail.

"Guess this is as good as it's getting." She sighed and wandered in. The tall grass brushed against her waist. "This place looks just perfect for ticks. Fuck my god damn 'Good Day Off.'"

She wasn't the best at tree identification. She knew a pine tree from a deciduous tree, but these ones. The pine needles were too long for a short tree or too flat. Things seemed familiar, but not quite right. Not exactly identifiable.

She was looking at a large yellow flower far ahead when the ground gave out again!

"SHIT!" She screamed as she fell.

The world stayed this time, at least. The sky stayed above her as the ground gave out.

Two long seconds seemed to span forever as she fell, down, away from the trees.

It was a damn pitfall! The broken branches, leaves and grass that had been hiding it fell with her, suspended in the air for two seconds.

She fell squarely on her pack. The impact crushing her chest like a giant fist of pain.

She groaned and curled on her side. Mud. The thick smell of mud enveloped her.

She wanted to moan, "my backpack!" but there was no air for her just yet.

(God damn it! I really like this backpack!)

She didn't want to look at it. It was certainly covered in mud.

She'd had it for almost 6 months, and she had been able to keep it so clean that it still had looked new.

"Well fuck." She finally said when she could breathe. She lifted herself out of the gripping mud and started looking for a way out.

A new smell. It woke him from his half-sleeping state. Not prey? He slithered a bit out of the mud, popping more of his face out, flicking the air. Slowly, he lifted his head, listening.

Hours later, Anna sat down. The cave had deep mud walls on most of one side, a track of mud and stone that eventually ended in a pool of dark water.

"I was not planning on camping tonight." She looked up through the broken pitfall hole. Pink sunset skies greeted her. "I don't think I'm getting out of this place tonight."

On one of the rocks, she had spotted something fabric. Maybe the last visitor's forgotten hoodie or something. It was folded neatly so she made sure not to touch it.

He watched her study his skin from the dark corners of his cave. He watched her sigh and pull her funny clothes over her head. She curled up in the mud and fitfully rested.
