Chapter Text

Anna woke up groggy and sore.

"God damn it. Fuck sleeping in the mud. This sucks."

She lifted herself and rolled her neck, wincing.

"This mud smell is going to be in my clothes forever."

She wiped the caked mud off of the side of her hand on her pants.

Sighing, she looked around.

"I gotta find a way up."

She'd never really been in cave before. She'd walked through one with a well-defined walkway as a kid, but she was in over her head.

She pulled out her water bottle and had a sip, looking up at the cave wall with the stalactites and stalagmites and little windows they made when they touched.

"Wish I'd brought more water."

She'd put stuff in the car and stuff in her bag "just in case," she got lost. Those supplies weren't *actually* supposed to need to support her for long. Grabbing them was more of a formality.

(I regret that now. I should have actually cared.)

She loved hiking. She loved being in the woods, with the birds and the little creeks.

However, she was not good at rock climbing.

The pile of rocks and slippery stone cicles didn't look welcoming to her. It also didn't seem like there was any way actually up over there.

That folded fabric sat over there. All white and clean-looking.

(That's kind of weird. I wonder what it's made out of. Who the hell left it here? Were they camping? Did they set that fucking pitfall? Fucking hunters maybe. No. It's not orange.)

Anna scooped her backpack up on her shoulders and peered at the fabric.

There were footprints in the mud.

(Looks like someone tall. Kinda bigger feet. Maybe a, caver. I think that's what they're called. The people who climb in caves.)

The footprints melted with big wiggly marks, like someone had a huge rope that they drug around with like, a backhoe.

Anna figured it was from the water run off making streams on the cave floor.

(Maybe it's like, a tall chick.)

Anna surveyed the mud wall again. It was just, big flat wall of mud and rocks. She traveled back, deep in the cave where it got tight and small and looked disparagingly at the pool of water that disappeared under the rocks.

(God I wish I'd bought one of those filtering straws. They just seemed so expensive and I kept forgetting to grab one when I had money. I bet that climber chick had one. I bet she knew about cave pools and stuff.)

After a few hours of exploring back and forth, Anna pulled out one of her granola bars. She tried so hard to open it without getting mud on it from her dirty hands.

"This fucking sucks." She sighed as she bit into it.
