Chapter 5: Day 4
Summary:

    Anna becomes aware of muted noise in the cave and begins hiding in a muddy little cave she builds. She begins coughing.

Chapter Text

Anna becomes aware of muted noise in the cave and begins hiding in a muddy little cave she builds. She begins coughing.

On the fourth day, Anna woke up coughing.

(Damn it! I'm getting a cold!)

Hunger tore through her and she groaned.

(I gotta get HOME)

She unfolded, determined to get home! She sighed as she lifted herself off the cold

mud where she had slept.

“Hey.” She nodded at the fabric. It was a reminder that she wasn’t alone. Someone

was here. Some human had been here, and got out. Maybe some human would

come down and help her up. Maybe it would be someone who knew how to get out

of places like this. A skilled person, eager to help.

 

Anna’s mind played these favorable scenarios for her as she ignored her hunger

and pain and tried again to climb the rock wall. Trying to get up the mud wall was

gross and slippery, but when she fell from the rock wall, she ended up with gashes

and scrapes.

 

She was about three feet off the ground when she heard something!

(Yes! Maybe someone is coming!)

She hopped down and followed the sound! It was coming from somewhere around

the mud wall! No, behind the mud wall. No, that was big.

(That’s not a person!) She realized.

 

Suddenly, fear rippled through her.

The thing seemed to slip around the whole outside of her cave, the loud whisperes

echoing over the water.

Her heart pounded.

 

(What is that!? God! I gotta hide!)

Her wide eyes scanned the area.

(The mud! Yeah!)

 

She ran over to where she built the lowest edge. She had made a little hole. There

was a smaller cave in her cave. She dug more, making it taller and a little bigger so

she could squeeze in it and sit up.

A coughing fit took her. She climbed out and tried to stop coughing. Her eyes

darted back and forth. The sounds had stopped.

(I hope it didn’t hear me!)

 

She dug out her little hole as much as she could.

“I’m sorry.” She stared at her backpack apologetically. She gave the black and white

checkerboard pattern one last look before she sadly slathered the grey mud all over

it. The little purple pocket in the front got covered in mud too.

She sighed, hoping she could find another cool backpack again some day. She used

the backpack as a little door to hide the entrance to her hole. Then, she slept there,

sitting up against the muddy wall.
