Chapter 7: Day 7
Chapter Text

Anna didn’t know that it had been seven days since she’d found herself here.

She just knew it had been too long.

 

She willed herself to move, but she could only muster small wiggles.

In defeat, she put her backpack over the hole and curled into a miserable ball.

Her head burned and her body ached.

 

Salty tears stung clean trails down her temple.

This is how I die. She pondered sadly to herself. No one will know. No one will care and no one will ever find my body.

She thought about all the family that wouldn’t miss her. She thought about all the acquaintances and coworkers she dealt with.

 

She melted into the greatest despair she has ever sunk into.

 

She sobbed and coughed and curled deeper into her hole.

 

He could hear her sobbing, shoved in the wall. She’d been there for hours, just crying.

He was so confused. Normally, these noises would make him want to tear the creature out and devour it.

After the sobbing stopped, he got nervous.

He’d stayed in the shadows, not moving. He hadn’t wanted to scare her. Now, however, he felt compelled to go. He needed to do . . . something!

He didn’t know what to do. Gently, he slithered through the mud. It melted beneath him leaving a long divot behind him.
By the hole, he flicked out his tongue. He could smell her particular fragrance. She smelled warm and somehow soft.

He considered poking his nose past her backpack, but he reconsidered. Eventually, he found himself pacing back and forth.

 

She couldn’t see, but Anna was keenly aware of the new presence. It loudly fumbled around outside her hole of safety.
In the darkness, she could hear the mud. It was squishing and squelching beneath some long, flat sounding thing.

It sounded big and irritated.

 

It was dark in her hole.
Her face burned. Opening her eyes was a useless chore.

With quiet determination. She wriggled further into the wall, pushing the stinky mud away with her feet.
The air in the tiny hole had become thick and warm with her breath. She longed to peek out and grab a breath or two of chilly cave air.
She didn’t though. She stayed quiet. She tried her best to suppress her coughing and stayed as quiet as she could.

Eventually, she drifted off to sleep.

