Chapter 8: Day 8
Chapter Text

He couldn’t hear her anymore.

He couldn’t hear her anymore and it made him. . . FEEL FEELINGS! He wanted- he NEEDED to do something. He needed to know she was still alive.

He stared at the backpack with blazing eyes.

How dare it obscure his view! What was going on?!? What could he do?!

 

He remembered in his legacy when his father had taken his mother to a herbivore village.

 

Before he’d realized what he was doing, he was digging. Digging her out of her hole. His hands groping at the mud. What once brought him comfort and soliace was now his enemy. He flung fistful after fistful away from his prize.

 

In her deep fog of consciousness, she could sense the danger. She could feel the mud shifting around her. In her mind, she was freaking out, but she couldn’t move. There was nothing that she could do to muster energy to make her arm move, or to scream. She was immobilized.

 

A white sliver of her pale arm appeared through the mud. His heart shuddered at the sight. She wasn’t usually that pale.

 

His hand snaked behind her neck. His other crept under her knees.

She was covered in mud.

Carefully, he pulled her warm, delicate body out from her hole.

 

Up again! Up and up. She felt the ground leave her. Her hole was gone. After days and days of falling, she’d grown a healthy fear of heights.

 

As he rose up, she cursed in his arms. He smiled.

 

She was so warm.

 

With lightning speed, he bolted up, out of the cave.

The cool grey morning sun sprinkled over her frail looking body.

 

Crushing small twigs under his smooth belly, he ravaged the ground. Nothing else mattered in that moment. Nothing mattered but keeping her alive.

He inspected her as he sped off. Thick saplings scrapped his underside. They occasionally pulled his skin and made him bleed, but he was unconcerned.

Her skin was pale. So warm. Across the paleness of her skin were subtle spider webs of purple and red.

 

He slithered for hours until he reached his destination.

The greys of the morning yielded to the pinks of the dawn.

As the sunlight grew, it revealed how blue her lips were. By nearly midday, her fragility was terribly visible. His anxiety grew with the light.

Before he could think, he was at the door of the doctor.

 

“HEY!” He called through the hide across the doorway.
“HEY!” He shouted again. His voice was booming and full of anxiety.

He was about to pull the curtain back with his elbow when a slender, older male popped out of the hut.

 

After glancing at her pale hand, hanging down from the snake’s arm, his eyes grew wide.
“Quickly, bring her inside.”

Bo ducked and entered the hut.

He instantly felt better in the darkness.

 

“Put her here.” The doctor motioned to a tall bed of hides.

Bo did. Then he sat beside her. He stared at her smooth face and willed her to be better.

