Chapter 32: Found Chapter
Chapter Text

Chapter 1:
Day 1
Anna was sitting alone on a wire picnic table, eating her protein cookie.
The sparkles were happening. A migraine was on the way. She sighed and stood to walk home with her cookie. She had about half an hour to take pills before the pain would start.
There was an obnoxious cloud of black-and-white sparkles right in the middle of her vision. She rubbed her eye with annoyance.
Parts of the sidewalk blended into the spot.

The day was bright. The warm sun prickled on her skin. She hoped she could get inside where it was dark before it started to hurt.

The sound of her shoe echoed as it slipped off the rounded edge of the sidewalk.

The spot was getting bigger. She closed one eye to gauge how big it was. It was big. She closed it and opened the other eye to see where she was going, but the sparkles were there to.
(That’s kinda weird.) She pondered to herself.

She kept walking, squinting as the spots got bigger, more consuming.
The sidewalk dissapeared into blackness. Purples spiderwebs took turns exploding on the black background. Her foot missed the step and she was falling.
Vertigo! Dizzy! Falling, but the ground should have smacked her by now. Still falling.
It felt like her ribs flattened all the way when she landed on some long grass.
(What the fuck?) She thought. The pain was there now. Dripping from her eye over the top of her skull.
She curled in a ball.
She blinked, surprised that her eyes were open.
She still couldn’t see. The purple spider blackness blinded her.
(Where the fuck am I?) She pawed around the ground. (The park?)

The grass seemed spotty.
Crawling on her hands and knees she felt the ground. A soft pile over something gave way!
She was falling again!

The fall didn’t knock the wind out of her this time. It still sent shocking waves of pain over her.
The wet mud slowly accepted her, melting around her arms and fingers.

“Eeeeeeew.” She rubbed her eyebrow with the back of her wrist.
It didn’t seem like it had been a half hour, but the pain was intense now. She tried to paw around the cave.
There seemed to be a muddy edge to it.
She curled up and napped.
Sleep was about the only thing that helped her migraines.

Day 2

Anna yawned and stretched.
Things felt weird. The smell of her house.
(Wait, where am I-Oh right. I don’t know.)

Anna opened her eyes. Sunlight was trickling down in an uneven hole; the hole she had made when she fell through the pile of sticks and leaves.

One wall of the cave was slippery grey mud. Another wall was a pile of large broken grey-brown stones. A curved, textured wall crept up and above.

The humid air hugged her.

Thankfully, she had a few snacks in her backpack. She pulled out a granola bar. The sounds of wrapper crinkling back reverberated through the hollows between the stones.

She discovered a little area where some of the large rocks were submerged under clear water.

The area wasn’t very big, and it was kind of pretty, but she was so lost!

She went back to the spot that she fell through.
She hadn’t noticed at first, but on one of the large rocks there seemed to be some delicate white fabric.
(What? Maybe it’s body! No, it kind of looks folded.)
She edged closer. The cloth was neatly folded into a nice square. It seemed to have a pattern on it.
(Huh, yeah, guess it’s just someones, like, shirt or something. Looks nice.)
She peered at it, but made sure not to touch it with her muddy fingers.

She went over to sit in the dent in the mud that already had her butt mark in it.

As she chewed, she eyeballed the rock wall. Other than a few unserious attempts, she’d never climbed a rock wall before.
(A little belief in yourself goes a long way.)

She slid her hands against each other to knock the crumbs off. Still chewing, she approached the wall.

The next day, She tried scaling the muddy wall. For hours she slipped, over and over again. So she started scooping up ledges to climb up.
The hole she fell into shone above her like a prize.
The pain of hunger throbbed in her ribs.

She took a break from ledge making to stare in her backpack again like it would have restocked itself. Notebooks, sketchbooks, a pile of crumbs at the bottom, a cookie wrapper.

(Oh yeah! My cookie! I was eating that when I fell.)

Fragments of the day before unfolded in Anna’s memory.
She sighed.

(How did I get here?)
She recalled the path from her apartment to the convenience store. There was a park on the way, but she didn’t think there was any kind of cave around there.

She looked back over to the white fabric.
(It’s so pretty. I wonder if the last like, camper, or cave explorer person just folded it up and forgot it here.)

She grabbed the large, unwrapped crumb of cookie from the bottom of her bag. With a scrunched up nose, she inspected it for hair or lint.
(Considering I have nothing else, guess this looks edible. Edible enough, I guess.)
She took a bite and tried not to think about it much.
(Ok, let’s get this done.)
She put her hands on her hips. She was determined.

On the fourth day, Anna woke up coughing.
(Damn it! I’m getting a cold!)
Hunger tore through her and she groaned.
(I gotta get HOME!)

She unfolded, determined to get home! She sighed as she lifted herself off the cold mud where she had slept.
“Hey.” She nodded at the fabric. It was a reminder that she wasn’t alone. Someone was here. Some human had been here, and got out. Maybe some human would come down and help her up. Maybe it would be someone who knew how to get out of places like this. A skilled person, eager to help.

Anna’s mind played these favorable scenerios for her as she ignored her hunger and pain and tried again to climb the rock wall. Trying to get up the mud wall was gross and slippery, but when she fell from the rock wall, she ended up with gashes and scrapes.

She was about three feet off the ground when she heard something!
(Yes! Maybe someone is coming!)
She hopped down and followed the sound! It was coming from somewhere around the mud wall! No, behind the mud wall. No, that was big.
(That’s not a person!) She realized.

Suddenly, fear rippled through her.

The thing seemed to slip around the whole outside of her cave, the loud whisperes echoing over the water.
Her heart pounded.

(What is that!? God! I gotta hide!)
Her wide eyes scanned the area.
(The mud! Yeah!)

She ran over to where she built the lowest edge. She had made a little hole. There was a smaller cave in her cave. She dug more, making it taller and a little bigger so she could squeeze in it and sit up.
A coughing fit took her. She climbed out and tried to stop coughing. Her eyes darted back and forth. The sounds had stopped.
(I hope it didn’t hear me!)

She dug out her little hole as much as she could.
“I’m sorry.” She stared at her backpack apologetically. She gave the black and white checkerboard pattern one last look before she sadly slathered the grey mud all over it. The little purple pocket in the front got covered in mud too.
She sighed, hoping she could find another cool backpack again some day. She used the backpack as a little door to hide the entrance to her hole. Then, she slept there, sitting up against the muddy wall.

Day 5
Bo watched in awe of her tenacity from the deep shadows of his cave.
When she edged close to his skin, he couldn’t help but pull his long belly up underneath him to peer closer.

(I mean, I don’t think they’re coming back for it.)
She chewed on her dry lip as she stared at the forgotten shirt or whatever.
(Or, maybe I could find whoever it was? Am I just hungry? Is this something I’d be thinking if I wasn’t hungry?)

She hadn’t been brave enough to try the water either.

She pulled herself away and kept working on the slippery mud ledges.
The mud was slippery today. Maybe more than it was the day before. She hated it. Her new ledges melted and disappeared under her naked feet. She had taken her tennis shoes and socks off and left them in her little hole.

The day wore on.

By the time evening filtered through the ceiling, she was exhausted.
(I don’t even care. I’m not leaving her here.) She thought to herself, imagining some awesome hiker chick who had left her cool shirt here.
She grabbed the cloth and shoved it in her backpack before she shimmied into her hole for the night. She coughed and shifted in the night. Eventually, she pulled the thing out of her backpack and wrapped up in it. I was actually a lot bigger than she thought. A blanket, maybe?

 

Day 6
Worn to the bone, Anna woke up tired. But her vigor was renewed!
She listened closely. The sounds weren’t there.
(I wonder if it’s sleeping. Whatever it is. Probably gone.)

She began the day by scaling the ledges as fast as she could.
When she reached the top, she lept. She fell.
Scale, leap, fall.
Scale, leap, fall.
On and on it went. Desperately, she flung herself. Each time landing squarely in pain.
He watched on with growing admiration. Small thoughts started to bubble up around the very edges of his consciousness.
Help her, these new thoughts seemed to say.
How? He would ask them. And they wouldn’t know. He was in this alone.
Help her?
He pondered this.

 

Day 7:
The beginning

Anna didn’t know that it had been seven days since she’d found herself here.
She just knew it had been too long.
She willed herself to move, but she could only muster small wiggles.
In defeat, she put her backpack over the hole and curled into a miserable ball.
Her head burned and her body ached.
Salty tears stung clean trails down her temple.
This is how I die. She pondered sadly to herself. No one will know. No one will care and no one will ever find my body.
She thought about all the family that wouldn’t miss her. She thought about all the acquaintances and coworkers she dealt with.
She melted into the greatest despair she has ever sunk into.
She sobbed and coughed and curled deeper into her hole.
He could hear her sobbing, shoved in the wall. She’d been there for hours, just crying.
He was so confused. Normally, these noises would make him want to tear the creature out and devour it.
After the sobbing stopped, he got nervous.
He’d stayed in the shadows, not moving. He hadn’t wanted to scare her. Now, however, he felt compelled to go. He needed to do . . . something!
He didn’t know what to do. Gently, he slithered through the mud. It melted beneath him leaving a long divot behind him.
By the hole, he flicked out his tongue. He could smell her particular fragrance. She smelled warm and somehow soft.
He considered poking his nose past her backpack, but he reconsidered. Eventually, he found himself pacing back and forth.
She couldn’t see, but Anna was keenly aware of the new presence. It loudly fumbled around outside her hole of safety.
In the darkness, she could hear the mud. It was squishing and squelching beneath some long, flat sounding thing.
It sounded big and irritated.
It was dark in her hole.
Her face burned. Opening her eyes was a useless chore.
With quiet determination. She wriggled further into the wall, pushing the stinky mud away with her feet.
The air in the tiny hole had become thick and warm with her breath. She longed to peek out and grab a breath or two of chilly cave air.
She didn’t though. She stayed quiet. She tried her best to suppress her coughing and stayed as quiet as she could.
Eventually, she drifted off to sleep.

 

Chapter 2: Day 8

He couldn’t hear her anymore.
He couldn’t hear her anymore and it made him. . . FEEL FEELINGS! He wanted- he NEEDED to do something. He needed to know she was still alive.
He stared at the backpack with blazing eyes.
How dare it obscure his view! What was going on?!? What could he do?!
He remembered in his legacy when his father had taken his mother to a herbivore village.
Before he’d realized what he was doing, he was digging. Digging her out of her hole. His hands groping at the mud. What once brought him comfort and soliace was now his enemy. He flung fistful after fistful away from his prize.
In her deep fog of consciousness, she could sense the danger. She could feel the mud shifting around her. In her mind, she was freaking out, but she couldn’t move. There was nothing that she could do to muster energy to make her arm move, or to scream. She was immobilized.
A white sliver of her pale arm appeared through the mud. His heart shuddered at the sight. She wasn’t usually that pale.
His hand snaked behind her neck. His other crept under her knees.
She was covered in mud.
Carefully, he pulled her warm, delicate body out from her hole.
Up again! Up and up. She felt the ground leave her. Her hole was gone. After days and days of falling, she’d grown a healthy fear of heights.
As he rose up, she cursed in his arms. He smiled.
She was so warm.
With lightning speed, he bolted up, out of the cave.
The cool grey morning sun sprinkled over her frail looking body.
Crushing small twigs under his smooth belly, he ravaged the ground. Nothing else mattered in that moment. Nothing mattered but keeping her alive.
He inspected her as he sped off. Thick saplings scrapped his underside. They occasionally pulled his skin and made him bleed, but he was unconcerned.
Her skin was pale. So warm. Across the paleness of her skin were subtle spider webs of purple and red.
He slithered for hours until he reached his destination.
The greys of the morning yielded to the pinks of the dawn.
As the sunlight grew, it revealed how blue her lips were. By nearly midday, her fragility was terribly visible. His anxiety grew with the light.
Before he could think, he was at the door of the doctor.
“HEY!” He called through the hide across the doorway.
“HEY!” He shouted again. His voice was booming and full of anxiety.
He was about to pull the curtain back with his elbow when a slender, older male popped out of the hut.
After glancing at her pale hand, hanging down from the snake’s arm, his eyes grew wide.
“Quickly, bring her inside.”
Bo ducked and entered the hut.
He instantly felt better in the darkness.
“Put her here.” The doctor motioned to a tall bed of hides.
Bo did. Then he sat beside her. He stared at her smooth face and willed her to be better.

 

Days 9-11

It was obvious that her case was not an easy one.
The doctor glanced at the frighting snake beastman, sitting cross legged next to his female.
He hoped he could escape that snake’s death grip once his sick female passed away.
He didn’t imagine she was getting better.
“Quickly, get her clothes off. She’ll need washed.” The doctor told the snake. He wasn’t taking any chances. He was going to employ the snake.
As the snake busied himself with his female, the doctor put water on to boil.
~~~
Bo stared at her strange, soft shirt for awhile. The color was hidden beneath the mud caked on it.
She had accepted his skin; she had accepted him as a mate. She shoved it in her strange bag!
Still, he hesitated. She hadn’t seen him; they hadn’t met. He wanted to make a good first impression! He wanted his mate to like him!
Taking a deep breath, he slid the fabric up and off of her pale tummy. The underside of her shirt was black.
Her breath was shuddering, at best. Wheezy and raspy. Her pale tummy had been saved from excessive mud. It almost glowed in contrast to her dirt covered arms and face. She was lovely.
The doctor’s rustling noises barely registered as he stared at her. Carefully, he lifted her arms and slid the shirt up. Up and off of her. She coughed weakly.
The boiling water thickened the air. It mixed with the aroma of the doctor’s various herbs to create a chocking perfume.
The doctor had crushed some yellow seeds in a stone bowl and added a handful of animal fat.
The anxiety on the snake’s face was palatable.
“Here,” the doctor said, a bit more gently. “Wash her.”
He passed the snake a bowl of warm, soapy water and a bit of hide.
Bo took a deep breath. He dipped the hide into the bowl. The blonde hairs of the hide resisted the warm water, and only slowly sucked them in.
Her shallow breathing made her chest ripple in the low lit hut. Even in this awful state, she was beautiful. Her muddy face was soft and slightly turned. Her breath escaped her parted lips.
He reached the hide to her hand. The heat of her under his fingertips surprised him again. She was warmer then the water. They glided smoothly, leaving trails of clean flesh as they went. Soon, the back of her hand and fingertips were revealed. Cleaned like they hadn’t been in over a week.
Gently, he worked his way up her arm. Dipping the hide into the water, it dripping, then washing.
The doctor put his slender hand on the snake’s shoulder. The snake whipped around and glared at the doctor like he might eat him.
“I’m sorry,” the doctor started gently. “I need you to hurry.”
The snake straightened. He stared down at this beloved stranger lovingly. Longingly. He longed to give her time. He longed to talk to her. He longed to see her run in a field of blue flowers. He longed to run his finger along the side of her breast and see her shiver with excitement. He longed to embrace her. In the shinning sun. In the damp of a cave. In the water.
He dipped the hide again in the water. It was lukewarm now and much more comfortable for him to dip his hands in.
